From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 19:20:21 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] kosmickrisp: keep the MSL of a pipeline compressed

A pipeline reads the MSL it was built from again only to build another
Metal pipeline from it, for a render pass that binds fewer attachments
than the pipeline declares, and to write a pipeline cache. The text is
still the largest thing a pipeline keeps, tens of KiB for a small shader
and a third of a MiB for a large one, and the vertex shader holds a
second copy of the fragment text.

A shader now compresses each text once, when its Metal pipelines are
built, with Apple's libcompression. LZFSE gives MSL a fifth of its size
(LZ4 a third) and decompresses at over a GB/s; it compresses at 0.12
GB/s, which a pipeline pays once, and the vertex shader copies the
compressed fragment bytes instead of compressing them again. A build
from the text, a variant for another set of attachments or a pipeline
loaded from a cache, decompresses into a temporary copy that it frees
once Metal has the library. A pipeline library's precompile, which hands
its text to Metal's library cache and is destroyed, and a dynamic
vertex-input variant, which drops its text for the parent to regenerate,
never compress.

A pipeline cache carries the compressed bytes, and a shader read from
one keeps them compressed, so a cache is smaller by what the text saves
and loading it compresses nothing. The serialized text gains its stored
length and codec, and the cache UUID now takes in
KK_SHADER_BINARY_FORMAT, so an older driver's blobs are refused rather
than read as the new layout. A length a blob cannot hold is refused
before anything is allocated for it.

Twenty-four graphics and twenty-four compute pipelines of 19 KiB and 14
KiB texts hold 1.28 MB of MSL, now 0.25 MB; the heap grows by a third
less, and their cache is 0.63 MB instead of 1.67 MB. Compressing costs
about 9 us per KiB of text: 0.19 ms on a pipeline Metal already has
cached (2.5 ms), and within the run-to-run spread on one it compiles (36
ms).

test_msl_packing checks that the MSL of created pipelines is held packed
and that the heap holds the compressed size, that a cache is smaller
than the text and a cache loaded from it holds the same text, still
packed, that raster variants are packed and cached, and that a render
pass that binds fewer attachments renders from the packed text. Run with
--bench N it prints what N pipelines cost, for a comparison against a
driver that keeps the text. test_msl_pack checks the compressed form on
its own, including the bytes a cache cannot legitimately hold.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
